==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP|US}} {{IPA|/ˈsteɪ.pl/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"steI.pl/}}
* {{rhymes|eɪpəl}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etyl|enm}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|estaple||lang=fro|market, (trading) post}}, of {{etyl|gem|en}} origin, ultimately from {{proto|Germanic|stapulaz|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|stebʰ-|post, stem|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|gml|-}} {{term|stapol}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term|stapel||lang=dum}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|stapol||lang=ang|market, court}}, and to English {{term|staff|lang=en}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{obsolete}} A market center for a regionally produced commodity.
# {{obsolete}} A principal commodity produced in a region.
# A [[basic]] or [[essential]] [[supply]].
#: ''Rice is a '''staple''' in the diet of many cultures.''
# A recurring topic or character.
#* '''2010''', ''The Economist'', Jul-Aug 2010, p. 27:
#*: In most countries, rubbish makes headlines only when it is not collected, and stinking sacks lie heaped on the streets. In Britain bins are a front-page '''staple'''.
# Short fiber, as of cotton, sheep’s wool, or the like, which can be spun into yarn or thread.
#: ''[[tow|Tow]] is [[flax]] with short '''staple'''.''

